@* One per grid, dropped once right before the grid's own table-wrap/
   header-row (not inside it — the header-row is a flex row whose child
   count has to exactly match the data rows below it, same reason nothing
   extra ever gets added directly into .header-row; see the column-
   alignment bug in app.css's own history for why). Renders nothing at all
   when no column filter is active, so it costs zero layout space on every
   grid in its normal, unfiltered state.
   <GridFilterClearAll Filter="Filter" OnFilterChanged="StateHasChanged" />

   Was a single "✕ Effacer les filtres (N)" text link — Badr couldn't find
   it ("I didn't find it"), which makes sense: nothing marks where it'll
   appear until a filter's already active, and it says nothing about WHAT
   is filtered. Rebuilt as removable chips (Excel/Airtable pattern), one
   per active column, each naming its own column + value and carrying its
   own ✕ — no need to reopen a column's box and hit Escape just to drop
   one filter. "Tout effacer" only shows once there are 2+ chips, since
   with exactly one, that chip's own ✕ already *is* "clear everything".

   The outer div is ALWAYS rendered, even with zero filters (collapsed to
   nothing visually via the -empty modifier class, not removed from the
   DOM) — mafaliRowResize (app.js) snapshots which DOM elements belong to
   a grid's resize group fresh on every drag and floats them together via
   translateY. With the old version, this row only existed in the DOM
   once Filter.HasAny — so expanding a grid *before* any filter was set
   left it out of that drag's snapshot entirely; setting a filter
   afterward, while the grid was still expanded, then inserted this row
   fresh at its normal (un-transformed) position, which the already-
   floated grid was now sitting on top of. Badr, 2026-09-28: "the filter
   chips dont get resized... they become invisible" — exactly that.
   Keeping the wrapper permanently in the DOM means every resize drag
   captures it regardless of whether a filter happens to be active yet,
   so it always carries the right offset by the time a filter actually
   shows up inside it. *@
<div class="grid-filter-chip-row @(Filter.HasAny ? "" : "grid-filter-chip-row-empty")">
    @if (Filter.HasAny)
    {
        @foreach (var (column, label, query) in Filter.ActiveChips)
        {
            <span class="grid-filter-chip">
                @label: @query
                <span class="grid-filter-chip-remove" @onclick="() => Remove(column)">✕</span>
            </span>
        }
        @if (Filter.ActiveCount > 1)
        {
            <button type="button" class="grid-filter-clear-all" @onclick="ClearAll">Tout effacer</button>
        }
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required GridFilterState Filter { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required EventCallback OnFilterChanged { get; set; }

    private async Task Remove(string column)
    {
        Filter.RemoveColumn(column);
        await OnFilterChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private async Task ClearAll()
    {
        Filter.ClearAll();
        await OnFilterChanged.InvokeAsync();
    }
}
